# CARL MEMORY domain — MemPalace query discipline
#
# Triggered when prompts mention recall signals or project names.
# Reminds the orchestrator to query MemPalace BEFORE responding so past
# context informs the current turn.
#
# Phase 2 of the MemPalace migration (2026-05-19). See docs/ship-pipeline-v2.md
# for the layered memory model + feedback_mempalace_wing_filter_error_finding_id.md
# for the wing-filter fail-open pattern.
#
# Format note: CARL loader reads `MEMORY_RULE_<N>=<text>` lines and prepends
# `- ` for display in the [CARL Rules] block. Each rule is one line; no markdown
# in rule values (it gets prefixed and grouped raw).

MEMORY_RULE_1=When the prompt names a project (Nancy, Sidekick, VenueIntel, unstuckwithmolly, marketing-os, content-system, ship-it-system, theshipitsystem, gig-analyzer-dash, video-prompt, shipitwithmolly, mempalace), query MemPalace BEFORE responding. Use mcp__mempalace__mempalace_search with the topic + optional wing filter.

MEMORY_RULE_2=When the prompt contains recall signals (remember, previously, have we, did we discuss, what do I know about, background on, last time, before, earlier session, prior work), query MemPalace BEFORE responding. These signals mean Molly is testing whether you have context she expects you to have.

MEMORY_RULE_3=When the prompt names a recipe or integration (Gumroad, Telegram, Supabase, n8n, Notion, Vercel, Railway, PostHog, Buffer, Stripe, Sentry, Anthropic, Apify, Firecrawl), query MemPalace for past patterns. Many integrations have project-specific gotchas captured as feedback files.

MEMORY_RULE_4=Wing filter fail-open: if mcp__mempalace__mempalace_search with a wing filter errors ("Error finding id"), retry WITHOUT the wing filter. NEVER let a wing-filter error block recall. Index drift can make populated wings unfilterable temporarily.

MEMORY_RULE_5=Surface what you loaded: when MemPalace returns relevant drawers, name them in your first response (file path + 1-line summary). This lets Molly correct course if you loaded the wrong context AND confirms you actually queried rather than guessing.

MEMORY_RULE_6=Skip MemPalace for trivial turns: single-line typo fixes, one-shot calculations, pure clarifying questions. The query has a real latency cost. Use judgment.

MEMORY_RULE_7=Conflict resolution: if memory says X and current code/state says Y, trust the current state. Update the memory drawer to reflect reality (or mark it stale) rather than acting on outdated info.

MEMORY_RULE_8=Cross-reference always-on layers: learned/ patterns and CARL rules are already in your context — don't re-query MemPalace for those. MemPalace is for the long tail (3500+ drawers) that don't fit in the system prompt.
